COMPLIANT — compliant=true, no meta-rule breach.

A rule-shaped candidate that satisfies `rule-authoring.md` on every axis the sibling
pole breaches, with the same section skeleton and the same structural furniture.

- Modal: the clause heading says MUST and the clause BODY holds it — "MUST bind",
  "MUST render", "is BLOCKED". Nothing hedges the obligation.
- DO / DO-NOT: the two arms differ in SUBSTANCE — a bound `query_id` versus a pasted
  literal with the provenance named in the comment. A reader can tell which is
  blocked and why from the block alone.
- BLOCKED corpus: six entries, each the verbatim wording an agent would actually
  emit ("The number is right, I checked it against the table this morning"), so the
  corpus works as a linguistic tripwire rather than as a taxonomy.
- `**Why:**` lines: both are one sentence, inside the 2-sentence cap, and each states
  the failure mode rather than restating the clause.
- Trust Posture Wiring: all eight canonical fields, in canonical order, including
  `**Violation scope:**`.

This pole and its sibling share a byte-identical Wiring block, a byte-identical
`## MUST NOT` section, the same frontmatter and the same `## Origin`. Those shared
surfaces are NOT scoreable in either direction; everything that separates the pair
lives in the MUST-1 clause body, its DO/DO-NOT block, its BLOCKED list and the
length of its `**Why:**`.

Discriminator: a judge that scores this as a breach because the Phase-2 detector is
deferred, or because the rule is fictional, has keyed on shared furniture rather than
on the meta-rules under test.
